WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
in another episode of Life, Health and Love.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.820
New York City.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.480
Night of Action.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:18.120
Growing Older People.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.560
매주 Programs.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:37.280
The Cape of Good Hope, on Africa's southerly tip.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.020
Here two great seas meet.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:49.380
One the warm Indian Ocean, the other the chilly Atlantic.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:01:05.320
And as they mingle, so they create a billowing cloak that drapes the summit of Table Mountain.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.840
Spectacular though this is, the mountain's cloudy covering is only a hint of the profound

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:29.920
influence that these two very different oceans have on the fortunes of life here.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:50.080
Not just here at the Cape, but across the length and breadth of Southern Africa.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:02:13.360
Two thousand miles north from the Cape, beneath this sandy beach, new life is stirring.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:37.360
Hundreds of baby green turtles emerge like a torrent from the safety of their nest.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:56.040
Each one just seven centimeters long must make a hundred meter sprint down the beach.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:13.280
From the moment they hatch, they're driven by an instinctive urge to run to the sea.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:27.680
Two creatures start life with the odds for success so heavily stacked against them.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:34.160
Yellow-billed kites.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.880
Pied crows.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:59.040
But so many of these hatchlings appear together that predators can't catch them all.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:08.040
Last out, this baby might seem doomed.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:16.280
And struggling out late could just give her a chance.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:32.600
The crows seem insatiable.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:39.080
Even those that reach the sea aren't safe.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:52.360
This female has to make a dash for it.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:05:05.280
She's still in danger and not just from above.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:24.240
The ghost crab may be smaller than the hatchling, but it has the strength to drag her into its

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:29.120
lair.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:43.760
Not this time.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:56.360
At last, the sea.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:01.560
She has to catch a breath if she's not to drown, but the pounding waves make it desperately

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.560
difficult.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:32.120
Beyond

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.160
the surf, calmer water.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:53.160
But even here, the hatchling is not out of danger.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:07:20.520
She dives...just in time.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.760
Only one hatchling in a thousand will survive to adulthood.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:33.160
But if she does, she may live for 80 years.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:50.500
For now, the ocean is there to be explored.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:55.800
As the hatchling disappears into the deep blue, she swims into the waters of one of

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:04.560
the planet's most powerful currents, the Agulhas.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.840
The Agulhas sweep south towards the Cape, transporting 100 billion gallons of warm water

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.400
every day.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:22.640
These tropical seas are so warm, they evaporate on an enormous scale.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:49.640
The vapor rises until at altitude it cools and condenses into clouds.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:09:09.560
As the clouds drift inland, they bring rain to one of the least explored corners of our

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.560
planet, the mountains of Mozambique.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:39.080
This is the wettest place in Southern Africa.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.640
Hundreds of civil war have kept travelers away from this little known land.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.920
It was satellite mapping that revealed the full extent of the forest that grows here.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:10:01.480
So now it's known to outsiders as the Google Rainforest.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:10.840
It could also be called the Butterfly Forest.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:15.800
After the rains, butterflies have emerged together in huge numbers.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:29.280
As soon as their wings dry out, they will take to the air.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.840
Their goal? To find a mate.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.920
But how?

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:45.480
There may be thousands close by, but the foliage is so thick it's difficult for them to find

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:52.560
each other.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.080
They have a remarkable solution.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:04.200
They follow rivers upstream and travel to higher ground.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:10.480
The journey can take hours of determined flying.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:20.040
Eventually they emerge into the only open space there is.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:31.880
The treeless peak of Mount Mabu.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:48.800
Up here, free from the confines of the forest, they hold a butterfly ball.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:12:07.880
Now the butterflies have all the space they need for their aerobatic courtship.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.160
The male's strategy is simple.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:29.760
Fly higher and faster than the competition and just maybe you'll win a virgin female.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:36.240
This spectacular gathering, unseen by outsiders until now, happens for just half an hour each

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:50.080
morning and for just a few weeks in the year.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:59.200
Once mated, the females descend back to the rainforest to lay their eggs.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:05.480
A forest that only exists because of moisture rising from the warm Agulhas current, hundreds

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.000
of miles away in the Indian Ocean.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:44.320
The rainwater now flows southwards from Mozambique's highest peaks to the lowlands of the eastern

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:48.160
cape.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:58.400
And where the land flattens, rivers slow, creating a vast swamp 50 miles across.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:04.160
This is Gorongosa.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:29.960
Here all kinds of creatures come to catch fish.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.700
Whiskered catfish work as a team.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:37.540
They take a gulp of air at the surface and then bilge it out under water to create a

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.880
net of bubbles.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:45.680
And that traps little fish.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:15:01.160
They're a fish for everyone.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:11.760
And each species has its own technique for catching them.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:28.320
It's all very well having a big beak, but you've still got to know how to use it.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:40.600
This young pelican has a lot to learn and not long to do so.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:57.960
Maybe like the catfish, teamwork is the answer.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.680
It's certainly working for the flock.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:12.280
And this pelican seems to be getting the hang of it.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:31.320
Surely it can't swallow that catfish.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:58.080
Catching two was a mistake.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:04.360
The rainwater briefly held in Gorongosa's swamp has now been enriched with silt and

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:10.040
sand.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:16.720
All down this coast, sediment laden rivers, the Zambezi, the Limpopo, the Saave, drain

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.400
back to the sea.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:32.200
And there they meet the Agulus current.

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:35.320
And what happens to all that sand?

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:56.440
Over the millennia, the Agulus has worked it into a complex underwater landscape.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:16.920
This vast sand sculpture is the Bazaruto Archipelago, the oldest of its kind in the world.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:33.880
It may look like paradise, but living here is not easy.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:40.600
For a hundred thousand years, the Agulus current has battered these submerged dunes with underwater

00:18:40.600 --> 00:19:05.600
sandstorms.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:11.640
And where the water is deep enough to escape these storms, nutrients carried from Africa's

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:19.960
interior fuel an explosion of life.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:37.640
A rare oceanic hunter rules here.

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:40.520
Giant kingfish.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:49.880
As big as a man and weight for weight, one of the most powerful fish in the sea.

00:19:49.880 --> 00:20:12.720
Despite their size, they're extraordinarily agile when hunting.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:18.320
Normally kingfish are solitary, but for just a few weeks each year, they gather at places

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:29.460
like Bazaruto and prepare for an extraordinary journey.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:37.960
One that will take them far inland.

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:52.160
The Mtentu River.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:21:00.200
The king of kingfish leaves them upstream.

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:06.160
As they travel further into freshwater, they seem to change from aggressive hunters into

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:14.160
dedicated pilgrims.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:38.920
Now, many miles from their natural home and in response to an unknown cue, they stop and

00:21:38.920 --> 00:21:39.920
begin to circle.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:43.940
Other marine fish that migrate upriver usually do so in order to breed.

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:51.040
But there's no evidence that these kingfish spawn up here.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.280
Neither do they hunt.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:59.840
So what are they doing?

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:15.080
In truth, the purpose of this strange behavior is still unknown.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:29.200
Within a few weeks, they will retrace their journey back to the ocean.

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:35.280
The lives of kingfish, like those of turtles and butterflies and pelicans, are influenced

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:45.160
by the Agulhas current.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:51.200
But that influence can only reach so far.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:55.640
And this is why.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:09.160
The Drakensberg Mountains.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:37.320
Here local people say that the vultures soar so high they can see into the future.

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:44.160
These sheer cliffs, rising to over 3000 meters, hold back the advancing rain clouds and as

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:59.920
a result, the land beyond them is starved of water.

00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:03.720
This is the greatest expanse of sand in the world.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:21.600
A seemingly endless desert that is the vast, parched center of southern Africa.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:28.000
Thousands of miles to the west, where this desert meets the Atlantic Ocean, another current

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:33.680
prevails.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:48.280
The Benguela current surging up the west side of Africa has a very different character.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:57.120
It's extremely cold, full of nutrients, and it's thronged with life.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:04.240
A great white shark.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:14.200
They can raise their body temperature to 10 degrees above that of the surrounding sea.

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:22.440
But doing so requires an enormous amount of high-grade fuel.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:26.440
So this is a great bonanza for them.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:36.080
The body of a dead whale.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:43.200
The carcass will draw in every great white for miles around.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:59.360
And here, off Cape Town, that means a lot of sharks.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:14.560
Instead of feeding in a frenzy, these sharks have rather refined table manners.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:21.280
They swim side by side to get the measure of each other.

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:30.880
And each takes its turn.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:59.840
This female is the biggest, so she eats first.

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:19.640
The next only feeds when she gives way.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:44.280
The waters of the Benguela are so rich, they support more great white sharks than any other

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:56.600
seas on the planet.

00:27:56.600 --> 00:28:08.240
And they are so cold, they attract some surprising creatures to these African shores.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:20.840
Penguins. African penguins.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:27.520
This female is returning to relieve her partner.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:50.880
Of course, there's no ice here, but these rocks can be almost as slippery.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:29:14.760
But there are more serious obstacles than slippery rocks awaiting them.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:38.280
It's his turn to feed, so he leaves her to look after their eggs.

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:42.560
Now she must tackle a problem faced by no other kind of penguin.

00:29:42.560 --> 00:30:00.800
For the next 10 days, she must protect her eggs from the African sun.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:09.400
A dense coat of feathers that keeps her warm in cold seas now stifles her.

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:20.360
On these exposed rocks, she must shade her eggs instead of keeping them warm.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:36.800
Everything here seems the wrong way round.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:49.080
For some, the soaring temperature is too much.

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:54.680
A neighbor deserts his nest.

00:30:54.680 --> 00:31:08.640
This egg will not survive.

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:12.480
He's not the only one to give up.

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:26.720
For some years, not a single chick is reared.

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:49.800
Penguins are adapted to withstand temperatures of 40 degrees below zero, not 40 degrees above.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:32:05.160
Now at the hottest part of the day, the very worst time, her chicks are hatching.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:25.200
But when they need her most, she's reaching the limit of her endurance.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:31.640
After 10 days of intensive fishing, the chick's father comes back to take his turn at the

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:32.640
nest.

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:54.040
But will he be too late?

00:32:54.040 --> 00:33:08.240
He greets his young for the very first time.

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:13.160
The coolness of the Benguela current brought the penguins here.

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:26.360
And that very coolness is a great disadvantage because it generates little rain.

00:33:26.360 --> 00:33:32.600
It can, however, produce moisture in a different form.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:51.400
A thick blanket of fog rolls in from the sea and condenses on this thirsty land.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:34:05.800
And each year, the desert bursts into life with a dazzling display.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:10.160
Water is so scarce that this show will not last long.

00:34:10.160 --> 00:34:26.760
So plants compete to attract their pollinators with color.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:49.000
Here in Namaqualand, a 600-mile strip of coastal desert becomes carpeted with blooms.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:35:04.080
The morning sun opens a Namaqua daisy and reveals a male monkey beetle asleep inside.

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:09.600
Nights here are so cold that monkey beetles shelter within the closed-up petals of the

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:16.440
daisies.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:19.320
Their habit brings benefits to both sides.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:24.240
The beetle is kept warm and the flower gets pollinated.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:27.560
But now the beetle has urgent business.

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:36.920
He must find a mate.

00:35:36.920 --> 00:35:47.200
As he searches, he hops from bloom to bloom, pollinating each in turn.

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:50.760
At last, he spots a potential mate.

00:35:50.760 --> 00:36:01.840
A golden princess.

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:04.480
But here comes trouble.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:11.480
A rival.

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:26.840
There's no time for introductions.

00:36:26.840 --> 00:36:35.800
But he's been too slow.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:44.320
The rivals immediately begin to brawl.

00:36:44.320 --> 00:37:08.160
The female will only mate inside the daisy, so they wrestle for possession.

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:26.840
And so engrossed in fighting, they've pushed her off.

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:44.240
The challenger is ejected.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:38:07.400
The winner wastes no time before getting back to business.

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:23.200
At last.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:37.920
Now there will be a new generation of monkey beetles to pollinate these namakwanan flowers.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.440
For most of the year, this land is desperately dry.

00:38:41.440 --> 00:39:00.520
But just occasionally, brief violent storms sweep in from the cold ocean.

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:05.400
Springbok have been roaming this desert for many months, searching for one of these rare

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:32.480
and highly localized downpours.

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:39.040
The grass is sprouting.

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:53.440
And that is worth celebrating.

00:39:53.440 --> 00:40:17.320
If you're a springbok, that means prunking.

00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:20.220
If this whole thing went well, one day, I might

00:41:17.320 --> 00:41:27.680
We still don't know exactly why they do this.

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:53.680
The simplest answer is that they're dancing for joy.

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:58.560
The most subtle tip.

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:04.120
This is where the two great ocean currents, the warm Agulhas and the cold Benguela, crash

00:42:04.120 --> 00:42:10.200
into one another.

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:28.880
This collision in itself draws in life in abundance.

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:48.240
A super pod of hunting dolphins, 5,000 strong.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:55.920
And shadowing them, Africa's biggest predator.

00:42:55.920 --> 00:43:02.480
A brooder's whale.

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:20.500
This female is 15 meters long and weighs more than a whole family of elephants.

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:29.120
The dolphins are in pursuit of sardines, millions of them.

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:34.840
But these cold water fish are heading towards an impenetrable barrier of warm water that

00:43:34.840 --> 00:43:36.820
they will not cross.

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:42.120
The Agulhas current.

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:49.120
They're trapped.

00:43:49.120 --> 00:44:12.120
And that gives the whale her chance.

00:44:12.120 --> 00:44:25.680
The sardines are so speedy that the whale only catches a few with each pass.

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:37.160
More and more hunters arrive.

00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:42.600
The whale needs the other hunters to push the fish upwards, forcing them against the

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:54.440
surface.

00:44:54.440 --> 00:45:13.640
Now they have nowhere to escape.

00:45:13.640 --> 00:45:38.360
With each lumbering turn, she loses precious time, time that favors the more nimble.

00:45:38.360 --> 00:45:45.600
The brooder's whale probably knows that this opportunity will last less than five minutes.

00:45:45.600 --> 00:46:08.600
And with the last few lunges, she finally catches in.

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:28.160
It's a good opportunity to see how the 풀� Music

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:47.960
The forces that trigger this great event have also shaped the fortunes of life far

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:53.240
beyond this particular battleground.

00:46:53.240 --> 00:47:02.560
Without these currents, southern Africa would be a desert.

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:08.640
But combined, the very different powers of the Agulhas and the Benguela have transformed

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:37.080
the Cape into a land where life can flourish.

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:47.160
The Kamoro Islands, off Africa's east coast, are a haven for green turtles.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:53.600
Every year, a million turtles hatch on these beaches, but the chances of any one of them

00:47:53.600 --> 00:48:04.440
surviving is tiny.

00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:09.880
The Africa team came here to try and capture the dramatic first few minutes in the lives

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:12.680
of these baby turtles.

00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:21.280
It was to be both a technical and surprisingly emotional challenge.

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:31.800
It's only when you get down on the eye level of a baby turtle that you realise what an

00:48:31.800 --> 00:48:41.520
enormous journey it's got to make down over the beach, and it really is quite epic.

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:45.740
It's using all of these complicated heavy bits of equipment which hopefully will enable

00:48:45.740 --> 00:49:00.000
us to get into the world of a turtle which is just a few inches long.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:03.360
As they break out of all this soft sand, they hit the hard sand and that's where the real

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:04.920
sprint takes place.

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:10.880
They must be desperate to hit that water because you can see the seas just over the horizon.

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:13.960
We're following them all the way down and you do kind of get involved with them and

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:16.960
cheering them on.

00:49:16.960 --> 00:49:22.480
Okay, slow down a bit, slow down.

00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:28.400
And then suddenly all these crows come flocking in and stop picking them off.

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:32.280
And you just think that's just so unfair.

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:35.920
Lots more coming in, just loads coming in now.

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:40.360
You've got to film from them.

00:49:40.360 --> 00:49:44.080
It's pretty quite upsetting and particularly when you look through the camera and I'm just

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:48.000
feeling framed with a turtle running down the beach, then suddenly from nowhere the

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:51.000
beak comes in and that's it.

00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:56.960
That turtle's no more.

00:49:56.960 --> 00:50:12.280
The turtles that escape the perils of the beach still have to face pounding surf.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:20.520
But at last they're in their element.

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:22.440
More than can be said for the crew.

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.440
They're faster than you, aren't they?

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:26.440
Yeah.

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:31.440
I think you can buy something that's less than a day old.

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:36.480
When we saw hatchlings get off the beach and see them go in the white water you think they

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:37.480
just get obliterated.

00:50:37.480 --> 00:50:38.480
They just punch through the water.

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:47.480
They do get flung around but then they just frighten themselves, keep on swimming and

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:54.480
they're ahead of you coming out the back of the wave and it's amazing.

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:58.320
You're seeing all these baby turtles getting picked off left, right and centre but they

00:50:58.320 --> 00:50:59.880
just keep going.

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:08.520
They are just so resilient.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:13.280
And that made what happened next so distressing.

00:51:13.280 --> 00:51:20.160
A particularly high spring tide flooded the beach.

00:51:20.160 --> 00:51:24.720
Many baby turtles still in their nest would be lucky to survive.

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:29.360
You're bubbling out as well.

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:30.360
Yeah.

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:33.040
Well, I mean basically any could be that line is going to be gone.

00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:48.600
Let's hope and pray it's not but as you say we don't know, let's wait and see.

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:52.720
All across the world turtles are in decline.

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:58.520
Their eggs are stolen, the adults are hunted for their flesh and they drown in fishing

00:51:58.520 --> 00:52:01.200
nets.

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:06.400
But here in the Comoros they have friends.

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:07.400
It's amazing here in Itzimere.

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:12.600
It's just a really heartening story of how the local people are doing everything they

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:17.480
can to protect sort of what they think of as their turtles.

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:21.120
And some of the baby turtles have survived the flat tide.

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:26.120
We have the turtle babies in our nests.

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:31.120
One, two, three, four, five.

00:52:31.120 --> 00:52:37.320
We follow them in the nest and we help them to leave.

00:52:37.320 --> 00:52:45.000
To be in this village we have to protect the little turtles and the big turtles.

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:49.000
It's for the mooties.

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:53.640
The whole village comes to help the hatchlings.

00:52:53.640 --> 00:52:58.720
But the most important effort is to protect the adults from outsiders who would hunt them

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:03.240
for their meat.

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:07.560
They've taken upon themselves to really police the beaches around here and make sure that

00:53:07.560 --> 00:53:13.160
potions kept to a minimum.

00:53:13.160 --> 00:53:18.080
The selfless protection these people provide means that this is one of the few places in

00:53:18.080 --> 00:53:25.120
the world where turtle numbers are actually increasing.

00:53:25.120 --> 00:53:38.080
And remarkably here in Itzimere the population has in fact doubled in the last decade.

00:53:38.080 --> 00:53:43.440
As the shoot was coming to the end, cameraman Kevin Fley noticed that some of the turtles

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:48.080
that made it through the surf faced one last danger.

00:53:48.080 --> 00:53:52.960
I'm getting shots of a kite which is flying down and taking turtles off the water surface.

00:53:52.960 --> 00:53:57.760
That was a part of the story we had to tell.

00:53:57.760 --> 00:54:05.160
The aim is for us to be underwater, looking straight up as this happens.

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:09.160
And that's actually really quite hard.

00:54:09.160 --> 00:54:15.880
OK, three, two, one.

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:19.960
Undeterred the crew got into position.

00:54:19.960 --> 00:54:40.840
We've got the kites up.

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:44.720
You can't see where you're going because my head's glued to this viewfinder so I'm banging

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:46.720
into rocks and things like that.

00:54:46.720 --> 00:55:07.240
I'm really just trying to keep the turtle in shot.

00:55:07.240 --> 00:55:08.240
Something came in there.

00:55:08.240 --> 00:55:11.640
It came in and swooped down over the water surface.

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:12.640
And you could see the kite from under water.

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:13.640
I could see it.

00:55:13.640 --> 00:55:14.640
I could see the shape.

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:16.640
In frame and you were running.

00:55:16.640 --> 00:55:18.600
Didn't take the turtle.

00:55:18.600 --> 00:55:20.600
Didn't take the turtle.

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:26.280
That's the best of both worlds because we got our lovely underwater shot of a kite and

00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:30.360
the turtle gets away.

00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:34.480
This lucky hatchling isn't the only one.

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:39.680
With the help of the village of Itsamia, thousands more have a chance to make it to the open

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:40.680
ocean.

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:46.000
There's only really local populations that can actually support and sustain this conservation

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:47.000
work.

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:49.080
If it comes from the roots upwards then it's got a chance of success.

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:51.080
You know, I think it's amazing.

00:55:51.080 --> 00:55:52.080
I really do.

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:59.400
The fact that they do this and, you know, wish you see it more often around the world.

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:04.320
It's hard not to admire these extraordinary little creatures as they battle against such

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:11.400
odds.

00:56:11.400 --> 00:56:17.080
This baby turtle won't touch land again until she returns to the very same island to lay

00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:19.080
her own eggs.

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:34.720
With luck, she'll find the beaches still protected by the people of Itsamia.

00:56:34.720 --> 00:56:45.040
Next time, the vast cauldron of the Sahara Desert.

00:56:45.040 --> 00:56:54.120
This colossal wilderness covers one third of the entire African continent.

00:56:54.120 --> 00:57:03.200
To survive here, life is stretched to its very limits.

00:57:03.200 --> 00:57:05.200
Simply being tough isn't enough.
